import
	../../html

intro-lambdas html[]()
	- section "Lambda expressions",
		- p "A lambda expression consists of:"
		- bullets
			- "The {of_} keyword"
			- "A destructuring; the same syntax as for locals"
			- "An indented block"
		- p quote
			{fn-subscript} calls the lambda.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				f = of x nat
					x * 10
				log f[1], f[2]

		- p "An optional return type annotation goes after {of_}."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				f = of::nat x nat
					x * 10
				log f[1], f[2]

		- p "A lambda can be squeezed onto one line using {then_}."

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				f = of x nat then x * 10
				log f[1], f[2]

	- section "Lambda parameters",
		- p quote
			All lambdas take a single parameter.
			{br}
			If nothing is written after the {of_}, it means the parameter is {void}.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				f = of
					log "OK"
				f[]

		- aside-small quote
			Empty brackets {lbracket}{rbracket} call {fn-subscript} with an argument of {parens}.

		- p "As with locals, comma-separated names destructure a single tuple parameter."

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				f = of x nat, y string
					log repeat y, x
				f[3, "foo"]

	- section "Lambda types",
		- p "A lambda type consists of:"
		- bullets
			- "The return type"
			- "The mutability ({data_}, {shared_}, or {mut_})"
			- "A destructuring in parentheses."
		- p "This syntax is used for the type of {var-f} below:"

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				f nat mut(x nat) = of x
					x * 10
				log f[1], f[2]

		- aside-small quote
			The name {variable "x"} in the type has no effect and doesn't have to match the actual parameter name.

		- p quote
			If the lambda expression ({of_}{sp}{var-x} above) has an expected type,
			it doesn't need to re-declare the parameter type ({nat}).

	- section "Mutability",
		- p quote
			The code in a lambda can access and even modify variables outside the lambda.
			This is called a {em "closure"}.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				cur mut = 0
				f = of
					cur +:= 1
					cur
				log f[]
				log f[]

		- p quote
			There are different kinds of lambdas for different mutability levels.
			(See {intro-link mutability}.)
			{br}
			The closure of a lambda affects its mutability:
		- bullets
			- "A {mut_} lambda has no restrictions. This is the default for lambdas with an inferred type."
			- quote
				A {shared_} lambda can only have {shared_} (or {data_}) types in its closure.
				{br}
				It can't reference {mut_} locals (even just to read them) regardless of their type.
			- quote
				A {data_} lambda can only have {data_} types in its closure.
				{br}
				It also can't call any {global_} functions. (See {intro-link globals}.)

		- p quote
			Most lambdas are {mut_} when there is no need to restrict them.
			{br}
			{shared_} lambdas are used in parallel code. (See {intro-link parallel}.)
			{br}
			{data_} lambdas are rarely used,
			but may be useful in situations where the lambda must be stateless.
			For example, a comparator lambda used for sorting might be {data_}.

	- section "\"it\"",
		- p quote
			A lambda can also be written as an expression in wrapped in {braces}.
			In this case, the parameter is called {it_}.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log filter::nat[] 1 .. 5, \{is-multiple-of it, 2\}
				# Equivalent:
				log filter::nat[] 1 .. 5, of x then is-multiple-of x, 2

		- aside-small quote
			This kind of lambda can't take up multiple lines, so it's only suitable for simple expressions.

		- p "A braced lambda doesn't have to use the {it_} keyword; in that case it ignores its parameter."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log map::nat[] 1 .. 5, \{7\}

		- p "{braces} alone is a lambda returning {fn-new}."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log map::nat[] 1 .. 5, \{\}

		- p quote
			For simple lambdas, the braces are optional.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log filter::nat[] 1 .. 5, it.is-even
				log map::nat[] 1 .. 5, it * 2

		- p quote
			When braces are omitted,
			they are implicitly around the tightly-packed expression containing the {it_} keyword.
			{br}
			Such tightly-packed expressions are:

		- bullets
			- "Dotted function calls: {it_}{dot}{function "is-even"}"
			- "Prefix operators: {function "-"}{it_}"
			- "Binary operators: {it_}{sp}{function "*"}{sp}{num-2}"
			- "{fn-subscript} calls: {it_}{lbracket}{num-0}{rbracket}"
			- "Type annotations: {it_}{colon-colon}{nat}"
			- "Option forcing: {it_}{bang}"

		- p quote
			The {it_} lambda can contain any number of these tightly-packed operations:
		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/math/math

			main void()
				a nat[] = 2, 8, 18, 32
				log map::float[] a, (it * 2).to::float.square-root / 10

		- p "… though the above is just to prove a point and would be more readable as an {of_} lambda."

	- section "\"new\" interfaces",
		- p quote
			Defining an interface implicitly defines a {fn-new} function
			that takes lambdas corresponding to each interface method.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a i =
					name: \{"keen"\}
					number: \{1\}
				log a.name, a.number

			i interface
				name string()
				number nat()

	- section "\"with\" blocks",
		- p quote
			Many functions that take a lambda do some preparation, call the lambda once, and then return.
			{br}
			To make that intention clear, there is a {with_} keyword providing a syntax for it.
			{with_} blocks provide a syntax to make that intention clear.

		- p "A common use of {with_} is to build a collection using {fn-build}."

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				xs nat[] = with out in build
					out ~= 1
					out ~= 2
				log xs

		- p quote
			The above is equivalent to calling
			{fn-with-block}{sp}{fn-build}{comma}{of_}{sp}{variable "out"}{sp}{code "..."}

		- p quote
			A type annotation on the {with_} keyword applies to the return type of {fn-with-block}.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log with::nat[] out in build
					out ~= 1
					out ~= 2

		- p "A {fn-with-block} function can be defined like so:"

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log with in log-start-and-end "a"
					log "computing a"
					"result a"
				log with in log-start-and-end "b"
					log "computing b"
					"result b"

			log-start-and-end record(name string)
			with-block[t] t(a log-start-and-end, f t mut())
				log "start \{a.name\}"
				finally log "end \{a.name\}"
				f[]

		- aside-small quote
			Above, the lambda takes a {void} parameter, so there is nothing written between {with_} and {in_}.

		- p quote
			It's not unusual to define a type whose only purpose is to be the first argument to {fn-with-block}.
			{type "log-start-and-end"} above is an example.
